// The `CosTrading::Lookup` subset `crates/orbweaver-giop/src/trading_server.rs`
// serves, written as a contract.
//
// # Why this file exists
//
// D022 T4 put `CosTrading::Lookup::query` on the wire, on our POA, answering
// with a nil `OfferIterator` for every result that fits and refusing the rest
// by name. It could not be added to `docs/SERVICES-COVERAGE.md` §8, and the
// batch that landed it stopped at the right wall: **the sweep derives its
// operation list by parsing IDL from our own corpus, and no first-party
// `CosTrading` contract existed.** D030 §5 L4 names that as the prerequisite
// every future target hits, not just this one. This file is it.
//
// # Provenance, which is the whole design constraint here
//
// **Written from the OMG Trading Object Service specification** — the
// `CosTrading` module's published declarations: `Lookup`, its three base
// attribute interfaces, and the types and exceptions `query` names. Those
// names, types and exception members are OMG's, published, and implementing a
// published specification ourselves is the first-party category `CLAUDE.md`
// draws; the ORB core is first-party for exactly the same reason.
//
// omniORB ships a `CosTrading.idl` and it is **LGPL**. It was not copied, not
// retyped from, not adapted, and not diffed into existence. Under clause (b) of
// the licensing boundary — invoking an external program and reading its text
// output — `omniidl` was run over **this file** as the conformance oracle, the
// same use every other corpus file gets, and `spikes/trading_client.py` already
// checks this contract's repository ids by *running* omniidl-generated stubs.
// Neither reads omniORB's own `CosTrading.idl` into this one.
//
// *OMG 명세에서 직접 썼다. omniORB의 `CosTrading.idl`은 LGPL이며 복사·전사·개작·
// 대조 중 어느 것도 하지 않았다. 재타이핑은 세탁이 아니다.*
//
// # Scope: what is served, and nothing wider
//
// D022 §7 forbids widening and it is still binding, so this contract is the
// **import side only**:
//
//   * `Lookup::query`, with its full signature — six in-parameters, three
//     out-parameters — and the exceptions the servant actually raises.
//   * `TraderComponents`, `SupportAttributes`, `ImportAttributes`, whose
//     twenty readonly attributes the servant answers every one of. A trader
//     that does not answer them is a trader a foreign client cannot
//     characterise, which is why they are here in full while everything else
//     is not.
//
// Deliberately absent, each because the servant does not serve it: `Register`
// (the export side), `Admin`, `Link`, `Proxy`, and any
// `CosTradingRepos::ServiceTypeRepository` — D022 T3 landed the service *type*
// and deliberately not the repository. Declaring their operations would make
// `spikes/service_sweep.sh` report a gap that is really a scope decision, which
// is the opposite of what this file is for.
//
// # The five interfaces declared as bare names, and why that is the honest form
//
// `Register`, `Link`, `Proxy`, `Admin` and `OfferIterator` appear below with
// **empty bodies**. Every one of them is a type this contract cannot avoid
// naming: four are the types of `TraderComponents`' attributes and the fifth is
// the type of `query`'s `out offer_itr`. The servant answers **nil** for all
// five — the specification's own way of saying an interface is not supported —
// and a nil reference is what makes T4's deferral conformant rather than
// short: when the matches fit `how_many` they are all in `offers` and the
// iterator is nil, so nil here always means *that is all of them*.
//
// A forward declaration (`interface Register;`) would say this more precisely —
// *defined elsewhere*, which is exactly the truth — and it was measured rather
// than assumed before being turned down. **Both front ends accept an undefined
// one**: `omniidl -b dump` exits 0 with no diagnostic, `orbweaver-idl`'s
// `collect_definition` introduces the name and never revisits whether a body
// arrived, and `corpus/services/ir-subset.idl`'s `interface ModuleDef;` has
// relied on that tolerance for as long as it has existed. It is still a
// tolerance and not a licence: IDL requires a forward-declared interface to be
// **defined later in the same specification**, so an undefined one is
// non-conformant in a way a stricter compiler may refuse.
//
// This file is a *prerequisite for other targets* (D030 §5 L4) rather than
// coverage material, so it is the wrong place to spend a conformance tolerance.
// An empty body is conformant everywhere and is the same thing every other file
// in this directory already does — `gen-naming-subset.idl` declares
// `BindingIterator` with only `destroy()` and omits the rest. The empty subset
// of an interface we serve none of is that convention taken to zero, and it
// keeps the sweep's operation count equal to the served operation count either
// way.
//
// `interface Lookup;` below *is* a forward declaration and a fully conformant
// one, because `Lookup` is defined at the bottom of this file —
// `TraderComponents` needs the type before then.
//
// *다섯 인터페이스는 이름만 선언한다. 서번트가 모두 nil로 답하며, nil 반복자는
// 언제나 "이것이 전부"를 뜻한다.*
//
// # Two exceptions the OMG raises clause carries and this one does not
//
// `Lookup::query` is published raising nine exceptions. Seven are here. The
// two that are not are `PolicyTypeMismatch` and `InvalidPolicyValue`, both of
// which carry a `Policy` — a policy's *value*:
//
//   * The servant never decodes a policy's `any`. `Policy` is
//     `{ PolicyName name; any value; }` inside a sequence, so the `any` is
//     never last in the request body and CDR gives it no length prefix;
//     skipping one means walking its `TypeCode`, which that crate has no
//     walker for. It therefore reads the first policy's **name** and refuses
//     there.
//   * That is sound because `orbweaver-trading` refuses *every* policy name:
//     this trader implements no import policy at all, its cardinalities are its
//     own constants, and it has no links, so no policy value can ever change an
//     answer. `IllegalPolicyName` is reached before a value is ever looked at.
//
// So both are unraisable by construction, and this file follows the rule
// `gen-naming-subset.idl` states for `CannotProceed`: an exception that is
// never raised adds a fault variant that can never be measured. They also
// carry an `any` **inside an exception body**, which no other contract here
// asks an emitter for. If a policy value is ever decoded, they land with it.
//
// The order of the seven that remain is the specification's own order, and it
// is load-bearing rather than cosmetic: `orbweaver-trading`'s `answer` checks
// in exactly this order so that a caller who sends both a bad type name and a
// bad constraint is told about the type name, every time, on every ORB.
//
// # Identity, and where the ids have to match
//
// `#pragma prefix "omg.org"` is what makes these the real ids —
// `IDL:omg.org/CosTrading/Lookup:1.0` — rather than a look-alike that would
// compare equal to nothing. Like the other two files here this lives under
// `corpus/services/` and not `corpus/golden/` for that reason:
// `pragma_corpus.rs` keeps the golden corpus free of identity pragmas.
//
// **The two exceptions nested inside `Lookup` are nested on purpose.** OMG
// declares `IllegalPreference` and `IllegalPolicyName` inside the interface and
// the repository id follows the scope, so hoisting either to module level would
// silently rename it from `IDL:omg.org/CosTrading/Lookup/IllegalPreference:1.0`
// to `IDL:omg.org/CosTrading/IllegalPreference:1.0`. No compiler catches that
// and a foreign client's exception dispatch is exactly what breaks. The
// servant's `ILLEGAL_PREFERENCE_ID` and `ILLEGAL_POLICY_NAME_ID` carry the
// scoped form for the same reason, and `spikes/trading_client.py` checks every
// one of these ids against omniidl-generated stubs by running them.
//
// # What is not in this contract because it is not in the protocol
//
// A property value is a `PropertyValue`, which is an `any`. The vocabulary this
// trader's offers actually carry — `id`, `specialization`, `placement_node`,
// `cost`, `latency_p50`, `latency_p99`, `load`, `mem_footprint`, `route_freq`,
// `residency` — belongs to the **service type**, not to `CosTrading`, and lives
// where the service type is declared. Writing it here would be inventing a
// contract OMG does not publish.
//
// Naming note, the dominant generation failure: the identifier clash rule is
// case-insensitive. The parameter and member names below are OMG's (`type`,
// `constr`, `pref`, `n`) and were kept for fidelity and for that. `type` is a
// **Rust** keyword and not an IDL one, which is the generator's problem and not
// this contract's — `corpus/golden/28-target-keywords.idl` is where that class
// is covered, and this file executes the `r#type` path incidentally.

#pragma prefix "omg.org"

module CosTrading {

  // Defined at the bottom of this file. `TraderComponents` needs the type
  // before then, which is what a forward declaration is for.
  interface Lookup;

  //@ ai_desc: An internationalised string; every name and expression here is one
  typedef string Istring;

  //@ ai_desc: The name of a service type an offer was exported under
  typedef Istring ServiceTypeName;

  //@ ai_desc: A constraint expression, which decides which offers match
  typedef Istring Constraint;

  //@ ai_desc: The name of one property of an offer
  typedef Istring PropertyName;

  //@ ai_desc: A list of property names, as a caller asks for or a trader refuses
  typedef sequence<PropertyName> PropertyNameSeq;

  //@ ai_desc: One property's value; its type belongs to the service type, not to the trader
  typedef any PropertyValue;

  //@ ai_desc: One named value an offer carries
  struct Property {
    //@ ai_desc: Which property this is
    PropertyName name;
    //@ ai_desc: What it holds
    PropertyValue value;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: What an offer carries, listing only the properties it has
  typedef sequence<Property> PropertySeq;

  //@ ai_desc: One exported service: what to call, and what is known about it
  struct Offer {
    //@ ai_desc: The object this offer is for; nil unless the deployment bound one
    Object reference;
    //@ ai_desc: The properties this offer has; an absent property is absent, not empty
    PropertySeq properties;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: The offers one query answers with
  typedef sequence<Offer> OfferSeq;

  //@ ai_desc: The name of one import policy
  typedef string PolicyName;

  //@ ai_desc: The policy names a trader reports having applied
  typedef sequence<PolicyName> PolicyNameSeq;

  //@ ai_desc: One import policy's value
  typedef any PolicyValue;

  //@ ai_desc: One import policy a caller asks the trader to apply
  struct Policy {
    //@ ai_desc: Which policy this is
    PolicyName name;
    //@ ai_desc: What it is set to
    PolicyValue value;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: The import policies one query carries; this trader refuses any name
  typedef sequence<Policy> PolicySeq;

  // `Object` rather than an interface: OMG declares it this way so that
  // `CosTrading` does not depend on the service type repository module, and
  // this trader answers nil for it in any case.
  //@ ai_desc: Where a trader's service type repository lives, if it has one
  typedef Object TypeRepository;

  //@ ai_desc: How far a query may travel across links to other traders
  enum FollowOption { local_only, if_no_local, always };

  //@ ai_desc: The service type name is not a legal one
  exception IllegalServiceType {
    //@ ai_desc: The name that was rejected
    ServiceTypeName type;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: The service type name is legal but this trader has never been told about it
  exception UnknownServiceType {
    //@ ai_desc: The name nothing is known about
    ServiceTypeName type;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: The constraint expression does not parse, or asks about something the type has no property for
  exception IllegalConstraint {
    //@ ai_desc: The expression that was rejected
    Constraint constr;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: A requested property name is not one this service type declares
  exception IllegalPropertyName {
    //@ ai_desc: The name that was rejected
    PropertyName name;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: The same property was requested twice in one query
  exception DuplicatePropertyName {
    //@ ai_desc: The name that arrived twice
    PropertyName name;
  };

  // The four interfaces this trader answers nil for. Named, never served —
  // see the header. `Register` is the export side, `Admin` the configuration
  // side, and `Link` and `Proxy` are federation, which a trader with no links
  // has nothing to say about.
  //@ ai_desc: The export side of a trader; this trader has none and answers nil
  interface Register { };

  //@ ai_desc: The federation side of a trader; this trader has no links and answers nil
  interface Link { };

  //@ ai_desc: The proxy-offer side of a trader; this trader has none and answers nil
  interface Proxy { };

  //@ ai_desc: The configuration side of a trader; this trader has none and answers nil
  interface Admin { };

  // Declared so that `query`'s `out offer_itr` has a type, and never served:
  // this trader answers every query completely or refuses it, so the iterator
  // is always nil and nil always means "that is all of them". Building one is
  // an object with a lifecycle per query, which D022 §7 defers.
  //@ ai_desc: Serves the remainder of an answer; this trader always answers nil
  interface OfferIterator { };

  //@ ai_desc: Which of a trader's five interfaces are available, and where
  interface TraderComponents {
    //@ ai_desc: The import side, which is this object
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute Lookup lookup_if;

    //@ ai_desc: The export side, nil here because this trader has none
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute Register register_if;

    //@ ai_desc: The federation side, nil here
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute Link link_if;

    //@ ai_desc: The proxy-offer side, nil here
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute Proxy proxy_if;

    //@ ai_desc: The configuration side, nil here
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute Admin admin_if;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: What a trader supports, which a client reads before it asks anything
  interface SupportAttributes {
    //@ ai_desc: Whether an exported offer's properties may be changed afterwards
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute boolean supports_modifiable_properties;

    //@ ai_desc: Whether a property may be computed on demand rather than stored
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute boolean supports_dynamic_properties;

    //@ ai_desc: Whether offers may stand for objects created on import
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute boolean supports_proxy_offers;

    //@ ai_desc: The service type repository this trader consults, nil if it exposes none
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute TypeRepository type_repos;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: The bounds a trader applies to a query, readable before asking one
  interface ImportAttributes {
    //@ ai_desc: How many offers a query searches by default; zero means no bound
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long def_search_card;

    //@ ai_desc: The most offers a query may search; zero means no bound
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long max_search_card;

    //@ ai_desc: How many matches a query keeps by default
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long def_match_card;

    //@ ai_desc: The most matches a query may keep
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long max_match_card;

    //@ ai_desc: How many offers a query returns by default
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long def_return_card;

    //@ ai_desc: The most offers one answer carries; a query matching more is refused rather than shortened
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long max_return_card;

    //@ ai_desc: The most an iterator hands over in one call
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long max_list;

    //@ ai_desc: How many trader links a query crosses by default
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long def_hop_count;

    //@ ai_desc: The most trader links a query may cross
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute unsigned long max_hop_count;

    //@ ai_desc: How far a query follows links by default
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute FollowOption def_follow_policy;

    //@ ai_desc: The furthest a query may be asked to follow links
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    readonly attribute FollowOption max_follow_policy;
  };

  //@ ai_desc: The import side of a trader: asks which exported offers satisfy a constraint
  interface Lookup : TraderComponents, SupportAttributes, ImportAttributes {

    //@ ai_desc: A preference expression, which orders the offers that matched
    typedef Istring Preference;

    //@ ai_desc: Whether a caller wants no properties back, some named ones, or all of them
    enum HowManyProps { none, some, all };

    //@ ai_desc: Which properties to return with each offer
    union SpecifiedProps switch (HowManyProps) {
      //@ ai_desc: Return exactly these, and refuse a name this service type does not declare
      case some: PropertyNameSeq prop_names;
    };

    //@ ai_desc: The preference expression does not parse, or orders by something it cannot read
    exception IllegalPreference {
      //@ ai_desc: The expression that was rejected
      Preference pref;
    };

    //@ ai_desc: An import policy this trader does not implement was named
    exception IllegalPolicyName {
      //@ ai_desc: The policy name that was rejected
      PolicyName name;
    };

    // `offer_itr` is always nil and `limits_applied` is always empty, and
    // both are answers rather than omissions: a nil iterator says the offers
    // returned are all of them, and an empty `limits_applied` says nothing
    // clamped the answer. This trader never clamps — it refuses instead, so
    // there is never anything to report there. A query that matched more than
    // `max_return_card`, or more than `how_many`, is refused with a system
    // exception rather than shortened, because a nil iterator over a
    // shortened list would be a false statement in the direction that loses
    // offers the caller cannot ask for again.
    //@ ai_desc: The offers of a service type that satisfy a constraint, in preference order
    //@ ai_effect: read_only
    //@ ai_authz: costrading.lookup.query
    //@ ai_precond: how_many is at least the number of offers the constraint matches, and at most max_return_card — otherwise the answer would need an OfferIterator this trader does not create, and the query is refused rather than shortened
    //@ ai_example: type "moe::Expert", constr "" and pref "" ask for every offer of that type in no particular order — an empty constraint means every offer, an empty preference means the specification's FIRST
    void query(in ServiceTypeName type,
               in Constraint constr,
               in Preference pref,
               in PolicySeq policies,
               in SpecifiedProps desired_props,
               in unsigned long how_many,
               out OfferSeq offers,
               out OfferIterator offer_itr,
               out PolicyNameSeq limits_applied)
      raises (IllegalServiceType, UnknownServiceType, IllegalConstraint,
              IllegalPreference, IllegalPolicyName, IllegalPropertyName,
              DuplicatePropertyName);
  };
};
